{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgavzZbnNtXFCt23y3yrQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I replaced the `sorry` in `factorialAcc_correct`, and `lean Main.lean` now runs with no errors or warnings.\n\n```lean\ntheorem factorialAcc_correct (n : Nat) : factorialAcc n 1 = factorial n := by\n  have h : ∀ (m a : Nat), factorialAcc m a = a * factorial m := by\n    intro m\n    induction m with\n    | zero => intro a; simp [factorialAcc, factorial]\n    | succ k ih =>\n      intro a\n      simp only [factorialAcc, factorial]\n      rw [ih, Nat.mul_assoc]\n  rw [h, Nat.one_mul]\n```\n\nInduction on `n` with the accumulator fixed at `1` doesn't work, because the recursive call changes the accumulator to `1 * (n + 1)`. So the proof first shows a more general statement, `factorialAcc m a = a * factorial m` for any accumulator `a`, by induction on `m`:\n- **Base case:** both sides reduce to `a`.\n- **Step:** unfolding the definitions gives `a * (k+1) * factorial k` on one side and `a * ((k+1) * factorial k)` on the other, and associativity (`Nat.mul_assoc`) closes it.\n\nThe theorem is then the case `a = 1`, using `1 * x = x`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":706,"cache_read_input_tokens":7971,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":706},"output_tokens":450,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4414c6f7-83b3-4b27-9f90-bb137bc0b291","uuid":"9c3d026a-3ee8-4303-b3f3-84bb29c88c42","timestamp":"2026-10-31T20:17:35.257Z","request_id":"req_011CgavzZJw8m1y9iU2BGLdF"}
